From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Midgy BALON <midgy971@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: pwrseq_simple: tolerate a missing reset controller

A mmc-pwrseq-simple node with exactly one reset-gpios entry and no "resets"
property (as the AIC8800 WiFi power sequence uses on the Trimui Smart Pro
 S)
takes the ngpio == 1 path, where devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared()
 can
return -ENOENT instead of NULL. The probe then treats it as fatal, so the
power sequence never registers and the SDIO WiFi is never power-sequenced
(mmc: "allocated mmc-pwrseq" never appears).

Treat -ENOENT as "no reset controller" and fall through to the GPIO reset
 path.

Signed-off-by: Midgy BALON <midgy971@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
index 4b47e6c3b..26bf18fa2 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
@@ -137,9 +137,13 @@ static int mmc_pwrseq_simple_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	ngpio = of_count_phandle_with_args(dev->of_node, "reset-gpios", "#gpio-cells");
 	if (ngpio == 1) {
 		pwrseq->reset_ctrl = devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(dev, NULL);
-		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
-			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
-					     "reset control not ready\n");
+		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl)) {
+			if (PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl) == -ENOENT)
+				pwrseq->reset_ctrl = NULL;
+			else
+				return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
+						     "reset control not ready\n");
+		}
 	}
 
